The Characters have 3,4,5,6,8,9. Maybe 3-4-5, 4-5-6 (needs W4, W5, W6), then 8-9... but need another tile. Not sure. \n\n- All Pungs: Do I have any pungs? No, all tiles are single except T9 (2), B7 (2), F2 (2). So maybe two pairs and the rest are pungs? Not sure. \n\n- Mixed Straight: Need chows in different suits. But my Dots (T) have T8 and T9, which could be part of a chow if there's a T7. But I don't have T7. \n\n- Seven Pairs: The hand has T9 (2), B7 (2), F2 (2), and others. If I can form seven pairs. Let's see: T9 (pair), B7 (pair), F2 (pair), F4 (single), W3, W4, W5, W6, W8, W9. That's three pairs and eight single tiles. Not enough for seven pairs. \n\n- All Terminals and Honors: The T9s are terminals, F2 and F4 are honors. But the Characters 3-6,8 are not terminals. So no. \n\n- Full Flush: All suited tiles in one suit. But I have Characters, Dots (T8, T9), Bams (B7). So not all in one suit. \n\n- Pure Shifted Chows: Four successive chows in same suit. Not sure. \n\n- All Even Pungs: If all pungs are even numbers. But I don't have pungs yet. \n\n- All Fives: Not applicable here. \n\n- Mixed Triple Chow: Three chows in different suits with same numbers. Maybe not. \n\nLooking at the hand, perhaps the best approach is to look for tiles that are not contributing to any high-value patterns and discard them. \n\nLet's consider each tile in the valid actions:\n\n- Play W5: W5 is part of the Characters. If I discard W5, I lose the possibility of forming a chow with W3-4-5 or 4-5-6. \n\n- Play W4: Similar to W5, part of multiple chows. \n\n- Play W9: Terminal tile. Maybe useful for All Terminals or other patterns. \n\n- Play B7: B7 is a middle tile. Could be part of a chow if there's B6 and B8, but I don't have those. \n\n- Play T8: T8 is a middle tile. Could be part of a chow if there's T7 and T9. But I don't have T7. \n\n- Play F2: F2 is a wind. Since the seat wind is East and prevailing is West, maybe not helpful for Prevalent or Seat Wind patterns. \n\n- Play W8: Part of W8-9, but no W7. \n\n- Play F4: A single wind tile. Not part of a pung yet. \n\n- Play W6: Part of 4-5-6 chow. \n\n- Play W3: Part of 3-4-5 chow. \n\n- Play T9: Terminal tile, could be useful. \n\nSince the melds are empty, the hand is all concealed. If I can form a concealed hand, maybe that's beneficial. \n\nLooking at the possible patterns, maybe the best is to aim for a Pure Straight or Pure Shifted Chows. But given the current tiles, it's not clear. \n\nAnother angle: which tiles are safe to discard? Looking at the unshown tiles, B2 has only 1 left, B3 has 3, B5 has 3. But I have B7. The other players' histories show that some tiles have been played. For example, the next player played B2 and T6, the player across played W7 and B5. So maybe B5 is already out? Wait, the unshown tiles show B5:3, so there are three left. So B5 is still available. \n\nBut maybe it's better to discard a tile that's less likely to be needed by others. For example, F4 (West Wind is the prevailing, but I have East as seat wind. F4 is West Wind, which might be needed for Big Four Winds, but I don't have other winds. \n\nAlternatively, discarding a duplicate tile like T9 or B7 or F2. Since I have two T9s, two B7s, two F2s. Discarding one of them might not hurt as much. \n\nIf I discard T9, I still have one left. If I discard B7, one left. F2, one left. \n\nBut which of these is less useful for my hand? The F2s are winds, but without other winds, they might not contribute much. The T9 is a terminal, which could be part of All Terminals. The B7 is a middle tile but not part of any obvious chow. \n\nBetween F2 and T9 and B7. Maybe F2 is the least useful. Discarding F2 would leave me with one F2, which could still be part of a pair or pung if I can get another. But since the seat wind is East, and I have F2 (South Wind), maybe not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W5 or W4, which are part of potential chows, but losing them might reduce my options. \n\nWait, the valid actions include Play F2. Since I have two F2s, playing one would leave me with one. But if I keep F2, maybe I can form a pung later. But since the melds are empty, I can't form a pung unless someone else discards. \n\nConsidering that I need to maximize my points, maybe it's better to discard a tile that isn't contributing to any pattern. The F4 is a single wind tile. If I discard F4, I lose that tile, but maybe it's not part of any high-value pattern. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard T9, I still have one left. But T9 is a terminal, which might be needed for All Terminals or other patterns. \n\nHmm. Another approach: looking for tiles that are not part of any potential meld. For example, F4 is a single wind tile, not part of any pung or chow. B7 is a single tile. T8 is a single tile. \n\nIf I discard F4, I remove a tile that's not contributing much. \n\nBut maybe the best is to discard a tile that is part of a pair or a possible pung. For example, F2 has two, so keeping one might be useful. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile from the Characters that is part of a potential chow. For example, W5 is part of 3-4-5 and 4-5-6. If I discard W5, I lose both possibilities. \n\nBut if I keep W5, I can form those chows. \n\nSo maybe the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any chow or pung. Like F4. \n\nSo the answer might be Play F4. But let me check again. \n\nWait, the valid actions include Play F4. The hand has F4 once. If I discard F4, I lose that tile. But since I have F2 twice, maybe those are more important. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard T9, which is a terminal, maybe that's better. \n\nBut considering the current state, perhaps the best is to discard a tile that's not contributing to any pattern. So F4. \n\nBut I need to check if there's a higher priority. For example, if I can form a concealed hand, maybe keeping certain tiles is better. \n\nSince all melds are concealed, I have a Fully Concealed Hand (4 points). But to maximize points, maybe I should aim for higher patterns. \n\nLooking at the hand, maybe I can form a Pure Straight if I have the right tiles. But I don't have W7, so the middle chow is missing. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W5, maybe I can reorganize the hand. But I'm not sure. \n\nAnother thought: the hand has W3, W4, W5, W6. That's four consecutive tiles. If I can get a W7, I could form a chow of 3-4-5-6-7, but that's not a standard chow. Wait, chows are three tiles. So 3-4-5, 4-5-6, 5-6-7. But I don't have W7. \n\nSo maybe the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential chow. For example, F4. \n\nSo I'll go with Play F4 as the answer.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for a Pure Straight or other patterns, but lacks key tiles like W7. Discarding a non-contributing tile like F4 preserves flexibility.\nReason: F4 is a single wind tile not part of any high-value patterns. Discarding it maintains concealed hand status and avoids losing critical chow tiles. The remaining F2 pair could still contribute to patterns if needed.\nAnswer: Play F4"}
